<Entry slug="souschef">
  <h2><span class="label">The question</span>“I have eggs, half an onion, and rice. Now what?”</h2>
  <p>
    Recipe sites assume you’ll shop for the recipe. SousChef works the other way around: tell it what’s in
    your kitchen in plain language, and it recommends meals you can actually make tonight.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="label">What I built</span>A small agent over a homemade knowledge base</h2>
  <Note>My first agent.</Note>
  <ul>
    <li>Scraped and preprocessed recipes out of PDF cookbooks into a structured knowledge base.</li>
    <li>Built the agent on <strong>Google Vertex AI</strong>, using NLP and intent recognition to parse natural-language ingredient lists.</li>
    <li>Matched those ingredients against the knowledge base to return recipes you can make with what you have.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It taught me the lesson the UCLA football assistant (entry No. 01) is built on: the model is only as good
    as the structured data and tools you put under it.
  </p>
</Entry>
